/-!
# Generic freeness for finitely presented algebra-module pairs

Source: Stacks Project, Algebra, Tag 051S, Lemma generic-flatness-finitely-presented
(blueprint: lem-pur-fp-algebra-module-generic-freeness).
-/

@[expose] public section

universe u v w

namespace Module

/-- A finitely presented module over a finitely presented algebra over a domain becomes
free over the localized base after inverting one nonzero base element. The basis need not
be finite, and the base need not be Noetherian.

Source: Stacks Project, Algebra, Tag 051S, Lemma generic-flatness-finitely-presented.
(blueprint: lem-pur-fp-algebra-module-generic-freeness)

Proof:
1. Choose finite polynomial generators and relations for the algebra and a finite module
presentation. Lift the module relation vectors to the polynomial ring. The module is the
quotient of the polynomial free module by these vectors and the algebra relations times
its coordinate vectors (def-sc-finitely-presented-module).
2. Let R₀ be the unital subring generated by the finitely many coefficients of these
polynomials. It is a domain and a finite type algebra over the Noetherian ring ℤ, hence
Noetherian by lem-weil-pbc-finite-type-algebra-noetherian. The same presentations define
an R₀-algebra A₀ of finite type and a finite A₀-module L₀.
3. The coefficient inclusion induces R ⊗[R₀] L₀ ≃ L: maps into any R-module are families
of images of monomial coordinate vectors satisfying the same relations. All coefficients
belong to R₀, so the two presentations give the same conditions. Apply the scalar-extension
universal property (lem-weil-pbc-module-base-change-universal-property); bijectivity of
restriction on all such Hom sets constructs both inverses. No flatness of R/R₀ is used.
4. Apply lem-pur-noetherian-algebra-module-generic-freeness to R₀, A₀, L₀. Obtain a nonzero
a ∈ R₀ with (L₀)ₐ free over (R₀)ₐ. Its image in R remains nonzero.
5. Put H = Rₐ ⊗[R₀] L₀. The map H → Lₐ sends r ⊗ v to r times the image of v/1.
Its inverse sends ℓ/aʲ to a⁻ʲ times the scalar extension of the inverse image of ℓ under
step 3. The module fraction equality criterion makes it well-defined: any annihilating
power of a becomes invertible. The maps are mutually inverse on fractions and pure tensors
(def-weil-pbc-module-localization-away; lem-weil-pbc-module-base-change-universal-property).
6. Identify H with Rₐ ⊗[(R₀)ₐ] (L₀)ₐ by r ⊗ v ↦ r ⊗ (v/1), with inverse
r ⊗ (v/aʲ) ↦ (r/aʲ) ⊗ v. Fraction equality and balancing verify these inverse maps
(def-weil-ring-localization-away; def-weil-pbc-module-localization-away;
lem-weil-pbc-module-base-change-universal-property).
7. A basis of (L₀)ₐ gives a basis of its scalar extension: the isomorphism to the direct
sum sends r ⊗ (∑ cᵢ bᵢ) to the finitely supported tuple (r cᵢ), with inverse
(rᵢ) ↦ ∑ rᵢ ⊗ bᵢ. Only finite support is needed, not a finite basis index set.
Combine steps 5 and 6 to obtain the claimed freeness. -/
theorem exists_free_away_of_finitePresentation
    (R : Type u) (A : Type v) (L : Type w)
    [CommRing R] [IsDomain R] [CommRing A] [Algebra R A]
    [Algebra.FinitePresentation R A] [AddCommGroup L] [Module A L]
    [Module R L] [IsScalarTower R A L] [Module.FinitePresentation A L] :
    ∃ a : R, a ≠ 0 ∧ Free (Localization.Away a) (LocalizedModule.Away a L) := by
  sorry

end Module
